<span className="uw-eyebrow">MAYOR'S FIELD GUIDE</span><span className="uw-title">A good city starts small.</span>
<span className="uw-modal-subtitle">Urbanward simulates households, workplaces and connected infrastructure in monthly steps. There is no requirement to place every service on day one.</span>
<div className="uw-help-section"><span className="uw-section-title">01 / A street, a light, a glass of water</span><p>Choose Transport and draw a short local road on flat land. Place a wind turbine or municipal power station within two lots of the road. Add a water tower to the same connected street. Roads contain shared power and water ducts, so separate lines are only needed for connections beyond streets.</p></div>
<div className="uw-help-section"><span className="uw-section-title">02 / Let the neighbourhood arrive</span><p>Zone roughly 16 residential, 6 commercial and 8 industrial lots. Build no more than two lots deep from a road. Keep factories several tiles from homes. Press Play and watch foundations, scaffolds and buildings appear over several months. Residential demand needs employment, but vacant business plots can start a small town before the population arrives.</p></div>
<div className="uw-help-section"><span className="uw-section-title">03 / Follow the evidence</span><p>The City inspector shows demand from −100 to +100. Power and water totals are citywide: a disconnected district may be short even when the total looks healthy. The overlays and individual lot inspector show the local truth. Advisers prioritise your actual shortages.</p></div>
<div className="uw-help-section"><span className="uw-section-title">04 / Growth is earned</span><p>Low-density zoning can reach two development levels. High-density lots may reach four; level three requires 600 residents and level four requires 2,000. Utilities, demand and land value must still support an upgrade. Offices need customers, factories need workers, and higher-level industry benefits from education. Mixed-use lots provide both housing and employment.</p></div>
<div className="uw-help-section"><span className="uw-section-title">05 / Streets are a system</span><p>Traffic is aggregate road demand from household commuting and industry. The visible vehicles are representative, not individual simulated citizens. Avenues add capacity. Automatic local buses need a powered depot on the same road component. Open Transport in the top bar to plan named shuttle lines, order stops and assign buses. Planned mode serves only running lines; enabled buses cost $45/month each. Schools, clinics, police, fire stations and waste collection follow the road graph, with congestion increasing travel costs.</p></div>
<div className="uw-help-section"><span className="uw-section-title">06 / Give districts a history</span><p>Sixteen fixed neighbourhoods acquire identities through their buildings, environment and services. Inspect a lot, open its Ward panel, rename it and choose a planning focus. Buildings keep their construction-era style until redevelopment. Gazette petitions track traffic relief, recovery and heritage commitments over three months. Heritage preserves low development; green planning improves amenity; employment focus encourages business growth.</p></div>
<div className="uw-help-section"><span className="uw-section-title">07 / Read the small print</span><p>Construction is charged immediately. Taxes and upkeep settle monthly. Budget forecasts exclude one-time construction costs and milestone grants. Undo stores ten construction strokes and expires at the next month. Green preview outlines show valid changes; existing development is not silently overwritten by another use. Over-budget plans do nothing.</p></div>
<div className="uw-help-section"><span className="uw-section-title">Controls</span><p>Inspect: click a lot, drag to orbit and scroll to zoom. In build mode, click or drag to lay a stroke; roads use an L-shaped path and zones use rectangles. Camera buttons work in every mode. WASD or arrow keys pan; Q/E rotate; +/− zoom; Space pauses; 1/2/3 select speed; I inspects; R selects roads; B bulldozes; Z undoes; N changes lighting; Escape closes a panel or cancels the tool. Precise X/Z controls are in the Lot inspector.</p></div>
<div className="uw-help-section"><span className="uw-section-title">What is in this edition</span><p>Original procedural buildings, three landscapes, two map sizes, organic zoning, utilities, budgets, services, bus coverage, district identity, a newspaper, civic objectives, scenarios, save slots, optional fires and shoreline flooding. Day, dusk and night are visual presets; solar generation is based on monthly seasons, not the visual lighting preset.</p></div>
<div className="uw-warning-box">This is a substantial first playable, not a complete metropolitan transport simulator. There are no rail lines, metros, airports, individually simulated citizens, separate sewage pipes, earthquakes or online multiplayer. Audio is optional. The title screen shows a capture of actual Oceanview gameplay. City ratings explain happiness and nine other measures; open them from the top bar or City inspector.</div>
